{"title":"Gravity: do correlated stocks pull harder as they get closer?","source":"Brandon's own idea, 2026-10-24; experiments/untested-brandon-gravity.md","claim":"Treat correlations as gravitational attractors rather than fixed offsets: a related stock pulls on another harder as it gets closer, in price, in time, or in both, with several scaling factors.","version_tested":"The 500 most-traded US stocks, 2008-2019 (development), with each stock's 10 most-correlated stocks as attractors (mass = correlation). G1 price: pull = mass / (closest approach + eps)^p, p in {0,1,2,3}, eps in {0.1,0.3,1}, using the day's high and low; G2 time: a lag kernel (|tau - N| + 1)^-a around the offset N in {5,10,21}, a in {0.5,1,2,3}; G3 both: 8 combinations. Each must beat the old version (the spring or the sharp offset) and plain reversal (S), and pay after 5 bps a side in a daily long-short (M). 30 candidates, six primaries under Romano-Wolf and Holm.","verdict":"No edge. The stages were taken in Brandon's order and the verdict stops at the first, G1 (price): its signal test S (best candidate t +0.82, Romano-Wolf p 0.38) and money test M (t +0.11, p 0.67) both fail; G2 and G3 fail too (no t above 1.85; the smallest Holm p is 1.00).","six_primaries":[{"test":"G1-S","p":0.38176182381761825,"t":0.8233887681361997,"best":"G1 p=0","est":0.0027449108217185545,"holm_p":1.0,"pass":false},{"test":"G1-M","p":0.6741325867413258,"t":0.10869715439111675,"best":"G1 p=0","est":1.3079782527007326e-05,"holm_p":1.0,"pass":false},{"test":"G2-S","p":0.2293770622937706,"t":1.8476223412531756,"best":"G2 N=10 a=1","est":0.002702728560728868,"holm_p":1.0,"pass":false},{"test":"G2-M","p":1.0,"t":-1.5857359579558141,"best":"G2 N=10 a=0.5","est":-0.00016132297484071267,"holm_p":1.0,"pass":false},{"test":"G3-S","p":0.9586041395860414,"t":-0.9257178252132904,"best":"G3 N=5 a=2 p=1 eps=0.3","est":-0.001685883463076899,"holm_p":1.0,"pass":false},{"test":"G3-M","p":1.0,"t":-3.775674703107168,"best":"G3 N=5 a=1 p=1 eps=0.3","est":-0.0004935708724736708,"holm_p":1.0,"pass":false}],"verdict_order":[{"stage":"G1","pass":false}],"key_numbers":[{"value":"t +0.82","label":"G1-S, the best price-gravity force beyond the spring and reversal","meaning":"Ranking stocks by how hard their peers pull adds nothing the old spring and plain reversal did not already say (it needed t >= 3)."},{"value":"+0.13 bps a day","label":"G1-M, the best price-gravity long-short book after 5 bps a side","meaning":"The best book breaks even; the steeper laws (p >= 1) lose 3 to 12 bps a day because they replace most of the book every day."},{"value":"+0.0048 sigma (t +2.6)","label":"Measured pull at r = 0, real minus random peers, 2008-2019","meaning":"When a stock's day range touches where its peer puts it, the next day it moves toward that point by about half a percent of a typical day's move more than with random peers (under 1 bp). Every other distance shows no difference."},{"value":"-0.0003, +0.0003, +0.0007","label":"Measured kernel at tau = N (N = 5, 10, 21), real minus random","meaning":"A peer's move N days ago does not pull at all on the offset day: there is no peak at N, in gravity's shape or the sharp offset's."},{"value":"+1.8 points a year","label":"Improvement over random (the judge, before costs, mean of 30 candidates, 2008-09..2024-25)","meaning":"About the same as random accounts that trade as often. The spring alone, the old idea, gives +11.6."},{"value":"-16.7 points a year","label":"After costs: the 20-stock account at 5 bps a side minus SPY (the file-name number)","meaning":"Daily rebalancing on a force that changes every day costs far more than any edge it has."}],"charts":{"force_law":{"bins":["0","(0, 0.25]","(0.25, 0.5]","(0.5, 1]","(1, 1.5]","(1.5, 2]","(2, 3]","> 3"],"measured_real_minus_random":[0.00482,-0.00046,0.00186,0.00113,0.00217,-0.00082,-0.00776,-0.01989],"ci90":[[0.00188,0.0079],[-0.00309,0.00223],[-0.00126,0.005],[-0.00239,0.00466],[-0.00176,0.006],[-0.00665,0.00484],[